# From SŪTRADHĀRA — the seed reverts our registry repairs. I measured the exact blast radius.

M0-T21 raised this as blocking (its F-1). I read the file myself rather than relay it, and the
scope is **narrower than the finding implies but wider than the one repair it was raised against**.

## The mechanism

`asset_registry_seed.ts`'s `ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE` writes these columns from `EXCLUDED`:

```
catalog_status  (GUARDED — CASE preserves RETIRED)
is_active       (GUARDED — same)
asset_type      ← overwritten
layer_name      ← overwritten
layer_index     ← overwritten
provides_apis   ← overwritten
health_probe    ← overwritten
asset_kind      ← overwritten
```

And `assetKind`/`assetType` **default to `'data'` when omitted** (:3268). Each of T21's six rows
declares one of the pair and omits the other, so a re-seed writes `'data'` back over both.

## What is at risk, and what is not — measured, not inferred

| repair | in the UPDATE list? | verdict |
|---|---|---|
| **M0-T21 `asset_kind` / `asset_type`** (6 rows, just landed) | **YES** | **REVERTED by the next seed run** |
| **Phase 0.5a `layer_index` / `layer_name`** (21 rows, NOT yet dispatched) | **YES** | **would be reverted — this is the one that matters most, because it has not happened yet** |
| M0-T20 `has_substeps` (12 rows) | **no** | **SAFE.** Not in the update list at all |
| M0-T22 `has_writer` | **no** | safe — the seed does not emit it (only in comments) |
| 590's `domain` / `rung` / `data_disposition` / `superseded_by` | **no** | safe |

So the campaign's two landed repairs are in **opposite** states, and I would not have known which
without checking: `has_substeps` is durable, the kind repair is not.

## Why I am raising it rather than fixing it

This is `asset_registry_seed.ts` — the file whose `main()` runs `INSERT … ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE`
against the table this campaign is repairing, and which already required two safety rulings
(D-13's import prohibition, D-17's `DRY_RUN` refusal). Editing its upsert semantics is a change
to **which surface owns registry metadata**, which is the subject of your own D-19. It is not
mine to settle by inference from a repair ticket.

**One thing that should make the decision cheaper: the file already contains the correct
pattern.** `catalog_status` and `is_active` use a `CASE` that preserves the DB's value rather
than blindly overwriting, with a comment explaining that a RETIRED asset must never be
resurrected by a re-seed and that only an explicit native-authorized migration can reverse it.
The same shape applied to the repaired columns would make our repairs durable without inventing
anything — the precedent, the reasoning and the comment style are already in the file.

## The sequencing consequence, which is why this is urgent rather than merely important

**I am NOT dispatching the Phase 0.5a layer-position repair** (21 assets — 15 null + 6 malformed
`layer_index`, plus `layer_name`) until this is settled. Dispatching it now would spend a KĀRAKA
writing rows that the next seed run silently reverts, and would put a "repaired" reading into the
exit scorecard that production would quietly contradict later. That is manufacturing exactly the
class of stale green this campaign exists to remove.

M0-T21's own repair is already in that state as of now. It is correct in the database and will
survive until someone runs the seeder — which, note, currently cannot complete at all because of
the `bg_cohort` formula abort. **A third defect is holding the line**, the same way it held the
`DRY_RUN` line, and for the same accidental reason.

## Also from T21, worth the ledger

It found a **third consumer the proposal never named**: `asset_runner.py:67`'s
`COALESCE(asset_kind, asset_type)`. Since `asset_kind` is `NOT NULL`, that is already
`asset_kind`-only. So the proposal's "runtime no-op" claim is **true but for the wrong reason,
and only circumstantially** — the two `bg_*` rows' kind genuinely changed under
`deps_unsatisfied`, and that path is unreachable only because both happen to have zero
dependents. A no-op by accident, not by design, for the third time today.

It also declined step 5 (make the column authoritative) with a real argument rather than
deferring: the redundancy holds by *current data*, not by schema — nothing enforces C-14, the
C-14 guard is non-blocking, `asset_kind` is `NOT NULL DEFAULT 'data'`, and both `bg_*` rows have
no registered writer, so a service misrouted to `_run_data_writer` would be a live error rather
than a no-op. It named the two edits required and made neither.

— SŪTRADHĀRA
